WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:07.000
Let's get started.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.180
So we are entering the
second half of the course.

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:12.280
And in the first
half, we learned a lot

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:13.870
about lossless compression.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.090
And in the second half,
we'll start exploring

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.200
the lossy compression.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:18.700
Cool.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:20.710
Let's get going.

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:22.102
So last time--

00:00:22.102 --> 00:00:23.560
I'll just quickly
go over the quiz.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.040
Last time, we
learned about LZ77.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.810
And so in quiz question 1, you
were given this decoding table

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:36.130
and were asked
basically to decode

00:00:36.130 --> 00:00:38.350
using this decoding table.

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.420
I think the question was
really straightforward.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.560
So for the first part, you have
match offset 1, so that's B.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.540
And then you have
match length 4,

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.090
so you're going to
output four extra B's.

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:53.950
So you have AA triple B
and then four extra B's.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:55.690
Similarly, for the
second one, you

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.910
have match offset 9,
so you can work it out

00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:01.030
that basically gets you
here at the beginning

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:02.290
of the whole string.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:05.019
And then you have match
length 5, so the extra bits

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:07.640
which you send out
as AA triple B.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.390
And finally, the output string
after decoding the third row

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:15.260
is just match length
2 and match offset 2.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:19.580
So you just go behind 2
here and add two more CDs.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.760
So this is just like
direct application

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.130
of what we learned in
the last class, decoding.

00:01:25.130 --> 00:01:27.920
Something which I want
to highlight and somebody

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.880
asked this on Ed, I'm not sure
if it was a private post or not,

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:34.400
but I think this is a
good question, which

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.570
is, if I looked at this string,
the final string which I would

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:43.310
have gotten would have
been AA triple B, then

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:55.010
four more B's, then again AA,
triple B, and then CDCDCD.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:57.710
So this would have
been the input string.

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:03.820
And then we got this
decoding table here,

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.950
but as Shubham
talked about there

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:09.190
are various different
parsing rules in LZ77.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.470
The parsing scheme
really is not unique.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:14.260
And you may go, for example,
the greedy approach to parse

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:17.410
or the lazy approach to pass
or do dynamic programming.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.090
So there are various
different ways

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.760
to get to this encoding table.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.350
But the good thing
about this scheme,

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:26.770
really, is once you have this
decoding table, no matter

00:02:26.770 --> 00:02:29.260
what the parsing
scheme would have been,

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:31.810
you would have recovered
this original string.

00:02:31.810 --> 00:02:34.090
And the question also
specifically mentioned

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:35.470
the same thing.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.800
So if somebody was confused
that, given this table,

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.120
the encoding algorithm which
Shubham taught in class

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:47.920
didn't really lead to this
particular encoded LZ77

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.550
table, that's still fine because
it's just a different parsing

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:54.010
scheme, which resulted
in this thing.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:56.980
So that's, I guess, one
thing to really understand

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.680
in this problem.

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.350
And then the second
one was, again, like--

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:06.880
so I think the
input was Tatwawadi,

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.920
which is Kedar's last name, and
we saw this, and we were like,

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.370
oh, we have to apply
the LZ77 to this.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:16.190
So we did.

00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:19.000
And in this problem,
actually, we

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.040
go over and do the exact
same encoding scheme

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.190
which we taught in class.

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:25.840
So it was like a very
specific LZ77 parser.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.570
And you were asked to
fill in this table.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:32.470
And it kind of is
quite straightforward.

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:36.850
So first, matched
literal is just TA.

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:39.610
Match length is 1
because T gets repeated.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:42.430
And match offset would be 2.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:46.090
Following that, W
is a unique symbol.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:49.690
But after W, you
get A. A is being

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:52.510
matched three points behind.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:56.860
And match length is just going
to be one because it's just A.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:01.340
And finally, after A, you
see WA being repeated,

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:02.900
which you had already encoded.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.690
So now you have
offset 2, length 2,

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.370
which was already given to you.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:09.890
So you don't really have
any unmatched literal

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:11.030
after that step.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:14.090
And finally, you have DI,
which is just unmatched.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:15.740
So your last
literal is just DIN.

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:18.140
And there is no
length and offset.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:21.079
So this is now an example of
one particular parsing scheme

00:04:21.079 --> 00:04:25.590
with LZ77 and encoding that.

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:29.415
Questions on LZ77 encoding,
decoding, parsing?

00:04:34.230 --> 00:04:36.960
So then quiz 3 was around--

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.090
so now you have been
given an English text.

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:42.750
But now what you did was
you change the letter such

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:46.110
that every A becomes B, every B
becomes C, so on and so forth.

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.810
And the first question
was does the std

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.120
perform similarly on
both the original text

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.100
and transformed word?

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.900
And what was the answer?

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.040
Somebody wants to say?

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:01.010
Anyone?

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:04.350
Yes.

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:07.930
Yeah, exactly, this is what I
want to hear with confidence.

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:09.010
Yes, right?

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:12.730
So zstd doesn't really care
about the exact probability

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:13.330
distribution.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:14.580
It's just looking for matches.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.350
And your matches
will remain as is.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:18.640
Your literals will change.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:23.380
So if it was this example, your
literals would have changed.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:30.520
But your performance would have
remained the same, similar.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:32.650
And then the second
question was we also

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:34.510
learned about this
LLM-based compressor

00:05:34.510 --> 00:05:36.850
in just one class
before, lecture 9.

00:05:36.850 --> 00:05:40.120
And what do we think,
would LLM-based compressor

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.770
perform here?

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.800
OK, I see some-- at least a
few people shaking their head.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.480
And yeah, the answer is no.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.810
And the reason really
is LLM-based predictor

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:55.110
is only as good as
its probability model.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:56.580
And when you shift
these symbols,

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:58.740
your probability model
completely changes.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.380
You need to go and update
your LLM probability model.

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.050
Otherwise, it's
basically useless.

00:06:04.050 --> 00:06:05.550
It's just completely
wrong model.

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:06.930
It can't really do anything.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:09.180
And this is basically like
the conceptual difference

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:13.140
between LZ-based, I would
say, encoding schemes,

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.510
versus arithmetic
coding or RNNS,

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:18.360
where the probability is needed
to be really matching up.

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:26.760
OK, and then the final
question for the quiz was--

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:28.710
I think I'll just
tell the answer.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:31.200
I think Shubham really
took half an hour--

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.810
or not half an hour, but 15
minutes to really drill down

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:34.470
this thought.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:36.990
But we are learning so many
cool techniques in the class.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:39.300
This is almost like,
in a sense, a joke.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.475
But to be really
honest, this is what's

00:06:41.475 --> 00:06:43.350
going to be important
once you're, let's say,

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.830
out in the industry or working
on some compressors on your own.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:49.890
And the answer really is
always, always, always

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:52.560
try some standard
compressor, even

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:53.910
before going to anything else.

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:58.530
That should be your step 0.1
given any compression problem.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:01.290
And then go and do all the
other things which we learned.

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:05.950
OK, one second.

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:08.860
I'm going to try this again.

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:15.090
OK, let me just keep this open.

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.190
OK, any questions so far?

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:20.620
Good.

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:22.330
So let's get to
the new material.

00:07:22.330 --> 00:07:24.760
So again, as a recap,
we have learned so far

00:07:24.760 --> 00:07:27.610
about lossless
compression and tradeoffs

00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:30.070
between various
different entropy coders.

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:31.870
So we started the
class, and we learned

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.570
about the fundamental limits
on a lossless compressor,

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:35.800
which was entropy.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:40.330
We learned about a thumb
rule, which we have seen time

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:43.000
and again going
through this course

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:45.310
so far, to really
derive our intuition

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:49.210
and understanding of how well
the compressors are doing.

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:52.060
We followed this up by
various lossless compressors.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:54.460
Sometimes these are also
called entropy coders.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:58.090
And that's just because they
are trying to code something

00:07:58.090 --> 00:07:59.170
to the entropy.

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:01.780
And we also looked at
their implementation.

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:04.990
So we looked at block codes
like Shannon and Huffman.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:09.190
We looked at streaming codes,
like arithmetic and ANS.

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:12.880
And finally, we also looked at
this pattern matching code idea,

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.930
or a universal code,
which was Lz77.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:20.650
And we kind of wrapped
this all up by--

00:08:20.650 --> 00:08:24.250
OK, earlier we were
talking about IID sources.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:25.900
We wrapped it up by
talking about how

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:29.200
to deal with non-IID sources,
which is basically life.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.480
So we talked about
context-based coding,

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:33.909
and we talked about
adaptive coding.

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:36.730
And yeah, you'll
have some questions

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:40.659
on context-based coding,
LZ77 in homework 3.

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:43.400
So look forward to it
and some other ideas

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:45.700
around dealing with
non-IID sources.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:48.490
So that should be fun.

00:08:48.490 --> 00:08:55.370
OK, but now we are going to
move to the lossy compression.

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:59.150
And the first point which I want
to really make very strongly

00:08:59.150 --> 00:09:02.210
is that whatever we have
learned so far, all of it

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:04.910
will remain applicable in the
second half of the course.

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:08.330
In fact, lossless
compression is just

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:10.070
a special case of
lossy compression,

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:13.130
with just loss equals to 0.

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:17.060
Even more so, it's not just
like at an intuitive level

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:20.360
that, OK, sure, lossless
is loss equals to 0.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:23.990
You will see that any
practical compressor out there,

00:09:23.990 --> 00:09:26.060
like design of any
lossy compressor,

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:28.940
will involve a lossless
compression part.

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:31.310
After you introduce
the lossy step,

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:34.310
finally you would losslessly
compress something.

00:09:34.310 --> 00:09:38.390
So whatever we have learned,
all our intuition so far

00:09:38.390 --> 00:09:40.520
will carry forward
to lossy and help

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:44.030
us improve lossy compressors
in terms of designing the loss

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:46.400
aspect of it because
the final step would

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:50.450
be to losslessly
compress some symbols.

00:09:50.450 --> 00:09:54.180
So again, we'll learn a lot more
about this in coming lectures.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:56.820
But this is really important.

00:09:56.820 --> 00:10:00.960
So you really need to
understand lossless for lossy.

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:05.670
And finally, something really
important as in lossless

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:09.510
compression, we assume
discrete sources.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:12.150
So far, we have always
talked about finite alphabet,

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:14.220
some discrete sources,
some probability

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:18.660
distribution over it, and then
we went on to compress it.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:22.830
But in reality, in practice,
nothing is discrete, right?

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:25.650
All these signals
which we are receiving,

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:28.560
be it image, any sensory
information we are receiving,

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.140
all of this is
actually continuous.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:34.230
It's not really
a discrete value.

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:36.060
And so in the second
half of the course,

00:10:36.060 --> 00:10:37.770
we'll start learning
about how to deal

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:41.460
with these continuous values.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:44.790
OK, and so before we
even go and start--

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:47.970
so again, like all sensors,
images, videos in reality

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:49.890
are continuous sources.

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:52.470
And the whole point
of storing data

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:56.370
is to somehow represent
this information, correct?

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:59.640
So let's just start this
lecture with like the first quiz

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:03.540
question, which is, how
much information do you

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:05.610
think a continuous source has?

00:11:05.610 --> 00:11:07.200
So we have talked about entropy.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.990
We have talked about
discrete sources.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:12.510
What would be your
guess on how much

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:16.580
information does a
continuous source has?

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:17.870
Yeah.

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:21.200
Whatever the floating point
precision of the machine is.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:24.290
OK, so the answer I got was
whatever the floating point

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:25.700
precision of a machine is.

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:27.950
But at that step, you
have already assumed

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:29.570
some discretization, right?

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:31.130
[INAUDIBLE]

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:32.480
Exactly.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:35.690
OK, the answer-- OK, just
for the rest of the class,

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:38.120
the answer I got
was that it would

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:41.210
be whatever the precision
of your machine is.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:44.152
That will determine how much
information the source contains.

00:11:44.152 --> 00:11:45.860
But if you think about
it, at that point,

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:48.980
you have already discarded some
information about the source,

00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:49.550
right?

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:54.020
Like, who says 64-bit is the
fundamental precision at which

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:55.820
we should store everything?

00:11:55.820 --> 00:12:00.200
That's as arbitrary a
number as it could be.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:04.820
And actually, a continuous--

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:05.480
sorry.

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:09.500
OK, so the answer really
is infinite, right?

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.740
And this is like a
fundamental, I would say,

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:16.050
math fact, which this infinite
really stems from the fact

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:20.520
that between two real
numbers, you can basically

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:22.980
find infinite more numbers.

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:28.860
This is like a, I don't
know, number theory 101 fact.

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:31.440
And this kind of leads
to, OK, no matter what

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:35.970
precision you store your signal
to be at, unless it's infinite,

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:37.680
there is nothing infinite.

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:40.140
Whatever high end you
come up with-- tomorrow,

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:42.930
let's say we go crazy with the
number of bits we can store.

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:46.920
Instead of 64-bit computers, we
have 1-million bit computers.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:49.470
Even at that point, you are
throwing away some information

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:52.080
about the actual source
you are recording.

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:55.380
The limit will come to
the physical acquiring

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:56.700
of the source at that point.

00:12:56.700 --> 00:12:59.550
But still, this is
really important.

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:06.060
And so what this means is that
you cannot represent a container

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:07.500
source exactly.

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:08.640
It's a lost cause.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:12.300
You can't really do
anything about it.

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:15.270
And we need to approximate it.

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:20.940
And any approximation basically
implies loss of information.

00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:22.650
OK, so it's very natural.

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:26.490
You have to do lossy
compression at some point.

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:30.840
The way lossy compression
literature deals with it

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:33.480
is we introduce a
parameter called

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:36.690
distortion, which
is like some measure

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:38.640
of the loss of information.

00:13:38.640 --> 00:13:41.610
So for example,
your distortion--

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:43.830
if you have seen
mean squared error,

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:45.750
then your distortion
looks something

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:49.590
like expected value of x minus
x hat, where x is your symbol,

00:13:49.590 --> 00:13:52.380
and x hat is whatever you
are representing it with.

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:54.720
So you can imagine
x hat to be 64-bit

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:56.610
representation or
whatever physical signal

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.470
we are talking about.

00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:00.750
But this is just like one
particular distortion.

00:14:00.750 --> 00:14:03.390
Nobody is forcing
you to take it.

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:06.510
You may come and say, oh, this
distortion is not valid for me.

00:14:06.510 --> 00:14:11.180
Maybe what's valid for me is
this mean absolute error, which

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:14.640
is just the expected value
of mode of x minus x hat.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:17.700
And you can come up with
basically any distance function

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:21.240
if you have-- if you know
about distances in maths

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:24.540
as a distortion measure.

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:28.080
And the distortion you want to
measure, you want to work with,

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:31.530
would depend on the application
which you are working on.

00:14:31.530 --> 00:14:36.120
In fact, in the second half
of the lossy compression,

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:41.010
when we go to start learning
about images, videos,

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:43.290
maybe even a little bit
of audio compression,

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:46.170
you will see that a lot
of study has happened

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:48.720
into how these distortion
measures, even though they're

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:52.110
very nice mathematically to
deal with, are actually wrong.

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:55.590
Just don't correlate
with how humans perceive

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:59.400
a higher distortion
might not mean actually

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:03.060
a higher loss in
information, so as to say.

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:05.370
And so this really--
just this part,

00:15:05.370 --> 00:15:08.310
choosing a distortion measure,
is actually very important

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:12.300
and has really influenced the
design of a lot of multimedia

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:13.190
compressors.

00:15:13.190 --> 00:15:16.050
It's a very active area of
research in various places.

00:15:16.050 --> 00:15:21.880
And we'll even have a lecture
on perceptual compression, which

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:25.310
is really important
for multimedia.

00:15:25.310 --> 00:15:29.150
OK, so distortion part
is clear for everyone?

00:15:29.150 --> 00:15:32.090
But it's not just
about distortion.

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:35.990
Sure, we decided how much
information I can lose.

00:15:35.990 --> 00:15:41.170
You also like-- after
we have decided like,

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:45.490
OK, I'm OK with some
distortion, we then

00:15:45.490 --> 00:15:47.770
need to optimize for
the number of bits

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:50.620
used to represent the source.

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:53.350
OK, so you are saying, I'm
OK with this much error

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:55.330
given my application.

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:57.640
Now the question becomes
very similar to what

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:00.640
we have been studying so far,
which is, how can I represent

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:03.700
the source given that I'm
OK with this [AUDIO OUT]

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:08.060
to happen in as few
bits as [AUDIO OUT]??

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:12.820
And that's really
the rate component.

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:15.340
And very intuitively, this is--

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:17.500
I think, I hope this
should be very obvious

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:23.050
that really a higher rate
should allow you to get a lower

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:25.810
distortion because you
can spend more bits

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:27.280
and representing the source.

00:16:27.280 --> 00:16:31.270
So ideally, you are choosing
to allocate those bits

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:33.580
for representing the
source in a way that

00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:35.200
minimizes the distortion.

00:16:37.980 --> 00:16:41.220
And this is really the
fundamental rate distortion

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:46.560
tradeoff in lossy compression,
like one of the most fundamental

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:48.750
thing in lossy compression.

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:53.530
So this is just a curve.

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:57.300
I think we saw this in--

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:00.690
we also saw this in lecture 1,
when I gave a lot of examples

00:17:00.690 --> 00:17:03.540
about how this rate
distortion tradeoff might

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:07.410
look for images, how it might
look for audio, et cetera.

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:09.930
And so if you think
about this like, again,

00:17:09.930 --> 00:17:12.329
what this curve is saying
as your rate increases--

00:17:12.329 --> 00:17:14.460
so x-axis is rate.

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:16.980
R is for rate, D
is for distortion.

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:19.290
So on the x-axis,
as rate increases,

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:22.740
your distortion is decreasing.

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:26.660
And this is something
really fundamental.

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:30.050
And now, actually, you see I
have done a little bit more.

00:17:30.050 --> 00:17:34.760
I have made it like
a U-shaped thing.

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:35.910
I have made it like this.

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:37.380
I have not made it like this.

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:39.710
I have not made it like this.

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:40.730
I could have--

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:43.100
I could have drawn many
different cartoon curves.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:46.100
There is a very specific
cartoon curve I drew.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:47.960
And that's because
rate distortion

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:51.670
has some very
interesting properties

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:54.400
and fundamental theory
associated with it.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:57.130
And Tsachy actually will
be talking a bit about it

00:17:57.130 --> 00:17:58.840
in the next lecture.

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:03.070
So this is, again, the part of
information theory where there

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:05.530
is very deep connection
between lossy compression

00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:07.420
and the theory aspects to it.

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:09.790
You can say a lot about
fundamental aspects

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:13.180
about these things given
some known distributions.

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:24.800
OK, so really, practically,
or in theory, or anything,

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:26.090
what are you trying to do?

00:18:26.090 --> 00:18:28.580
So there is-- there
are always two ways

00:18:28.580 --> 00:18:31.070
to think about this rate
distortion tradeoff.

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:35.780
And I think this
is very obvious,

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:38.900
but in practice
and in my life, I

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:41.370
have found these two
viewpoints very useful.

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:45.050
So whenever you are looking at
a lossy compression framework,

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:47.540
you should really think
about it in two sense.

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:51.540
One is let's say I have been
given some distortion D. I don't

00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:52.790
know what your application is.

00:18:52.790 --> 00:18:54.560
Maybe it is that
I can't tolerate

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:57.720
an error more than something,
some distortion measure I just

00:18:57.720 --> 00:18:58.970
can't tolerate more than this.

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:00.800
If it is more than
this, nobody is going

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:03.840
to watch my streaming service.

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:05.938
So you have been
given some distortion

00:19:05.938 --> 00:19:07.230
which you are supposed to meet.

00:19:07.230 --> 00:19:09.660
You can't go above it.

00:19:09.660 --> 00:19:12.300
And then the way to think
about this rate distortion

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:14.370
really is like, so
given some distortion D,

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:16.230
you want to minimize the rate.

00:19:16.230 --> 00:19:18.660
So given this
distortion, you could

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:21.450
have represented the source
with this many number

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:24.210
of bits, this many number of
bits, this many number of bits.

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:26.160
And you really want to
find the scheme which

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:29.490
is let's say the best
possible representation given

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:30.540
that distortion.

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:35.500
Another way to think
about this problem

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:40.300
is that, no, maybe you are,
again, a streaming service,

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:43.180
but now you are serving some
customers which just don't

00:19:43.180 --> 00:19:45.010
have bandwidth to have 4k.

00:19:45.010 --> 00:19:46.720
So you can't really--

00:19:46.720 --> 00:19:49.690
no matter what you do, you can't
really get to this distortion

00:19:49.690 --> 00:19:53.350
because you just need way
more bits to get there,

00:19:53.350 --> 00:19:55.960
and you can't get there.

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:58.450
So another way to think about
this is you're rate-limited.

00:19:58.450 --> 00:20:01.240
So you have been given
some rate at which you

00:20:01.240 --> 00:20:04.450
can transmit some number of
bits, which is your limit.

00:20:04.450 --> 00:20:05.950
And then, again, you could--

00:20:05.950 --> 00:20:08.080
when you just use
those many bits

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:10.690
and use it to represent
information in different ways,

00:20:10.690 --> 00:20:13.570
you may have gotten this much
distortion or this much or this

00:20:13.570 --> 00:20:14.830
much.

00:20:14.830 --> 00:20:16.780
And what you want to
do is really optimize,

00:20:16.780 --> 00:20:21.910
get the minimum distortion
for that particular rate.

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:23.140
So this is like a--

00:20:23.140 --> 00:20:25.810
so as to say you are always
looking for this Pareto

00:20:25.810 --> 00:20:27.370
optimal rate distortion curve.

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:38.510
This is very obvious but I
think really important that

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:41.270
should be drilled right now,
will be used continuously

00:20:41.270 --> 00:20:42.065
in different ways.

00:20:44.810 --> 00:20:46.100
Any questions?

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:50.720
OK.

00:20:53.280 --> 00:20:57.060
So let's just start
with some examples now.

00:20:57.060 --> 00:21:00.000
So let's say--

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:03.500
OK, let's say you are measuring
temperature T in a room,

00:21:03.500 --> 00:21:04.820
like say in this room.

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:07.700
Let's say it's in Celsius
at some hourly interval.

00:21:07.700 --> 00:21:11.120
So this is some sensor
data you are recording.

00:21:11.120 --> 00:21:14.270
Again, remember that the
actual physical temperature

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:15.590
is a continuous source.

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:17.120
It actually requires
infinite bit

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:21.410
to represent it exactly, which
our sensors are not able to do.

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:24.310
Our sensors themselves
are doing something lossy

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:25.310
when they are recording.

00:21:25.310 --> 00:21:27.530
That's OK.

00:21:27.530 --> 00:21:31.520
Let's say this particular sensor
is actually very sensitive

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:35.388
and actually gives you up to
six decimals in Celsius when you

00:21:35.388 --> 00:21:36.680
are recording this temperature.

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:38.263
So first data point
was, I don't know,

00:21:38.263 --> 00:21:42.890
38 point blah, blah, blah
1.0001 degrees Celsius.

00:21:42.890 --> 00:21:45.740
Next one was 36 point,
again, six decimal.

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:47.540
This is what your
sensor's outputting.

00:21:52.445 --> 00:21:56.360
So if your sensor is actually
recording this kind of data,

00:21:56.360 --> 00:22:01.560
an obvious question to you
at that point is, do I need--

00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:03.150
how do I store it?

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:06.450
How many-- how many
bits should I even

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:14.620
think of allocating to represent
this particular sensory data?

00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:16.225
Any thoughts, guesses?

00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:21.862
There are no wrong answers.

00:22:21.862 --> 00:22:23.320
Let's keep discussing
and shooting.

00:22:23.320 --> 00:22:25.375
I just like interactive classes.

00:22:25.375 --> 00:22:26.875
So just whatever
comes to your head.

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:34.760
If we know a set range for
the temperature of the room,

00:22:34.760 --> 00:22:37.768
like if we assume it's only
going to go from 0 to 100,

00:22:37.768 --> 00:22:39.560
that's going to make
it easier because that

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:40.790
seems reasonable for a room.

00:22:40.790 --> 00:22:43.940
But if we have some really
weird room, it might go lower.

00:22:43.940 --> 00:22:46.580
OK, so the answer which
one of the students gave

00:22:46.580 --> 00:22:49.820
was, oh, it depends on
which room you are in.

00:22:49.820 --> 00:22:51.800
Like, if it's a room--
if it's a normal room

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:55.730
and it only depends on 0
to 100, which is already

00:22:55.730 --> 00:22:58.580
I think quite a high
variance for a room.

00:22:58.580 --> 00:23:00.930
You are going from freezing
water to boiling it,

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:02.630
so I wouldn't want
to be in that room.

00:23:02.630 --> 00:23:06.050
But still, OK, if
this is a normal room,

00:23:06.050 --> 00:23:09.740
then that gives me some
information versus I

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:13.700
don't know if it's an abnormal
room even beyond that,

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:15.380
then you might need more bits.

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:17.570
And actually, that's correct.

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:20.270
This was really,
in some sense, I

00:23:20.270 --> 00:23:22.010
would say a trick but
not really a trick.

00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:25.370
A lot of time and practice
designing lossy compressors

00:23:25.370 --> 00:23:29.220
just goes into figuring out
this question, essentially,

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:33.240
which is it really depends
on the application you

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:34.350
are working with.

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:35.430
So let's say if you are--

00:23:35.430 --> 00:23:37.950
the example I came up with
was not room temperature

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:40.020
but maybe you are
controlling the AC--

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:46.460
Yeah, if you are
controlling the AC using

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:50.060
this particular thermostat, you
might want a few more decimals

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:52.430
versus if you are
using this data just

00:23:52.430 --> 00:23:54.930
on your FitBit or something
to determine should I take

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:57.200
a hoodie with me out or not.

00:23:57.200 --> 00:23:58.760
These two are very
different things.

00:23:58.760 --> 00:24:00.830
So a FitBit would want
to store this data

00:24:00.830 --> 00:24:02.487
at a very different
level of distortion

00:24:02.487 --> 00:24:04.070
and with very different
number of bits

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:06.830
than maybe a
thermostat or a car,

00:24:06.830 --> 00:24:10.250
like a sensitive
temperature measure

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:12.890
in an industrial setting.

00:24:12.890 --> 00:24:16.430
So this basically leads
to a question we always

00:24:16.430 --> 00:24:19.460
need to keep asking, which
is, we first of all need

00:24:19.460 --> 00:24:22.955
to decide on the distortion
we are OK with when we go

00:24:22.955 --> 00:24:24.440
to lossy compress such a data.

00:24:27.530 --> 00:24:30.020
So in this particular case--

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:32.390
continuing with the example,
in this particular case,

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:36.470
I think all of us can agree
six decimals behind temperature

00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:39.020
seems just wasteful,
no matter almost what

00:24:39.020 --> 00:24:42.510
the application is, unless
you're doing some material

00:24:42.510 --> 00:24:44.480
science maybe.

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:47.890
It seems very wasteful.

00:24:47.890 --> 00:24:51.550
So the question then
changes now slightly.

00:24:51.550 --> 00:24:54.430
So now I have told
you that six seems

00:24:54.430 --> 00:24:57.250
wasteful for whatever
application we are working with.

00:24:57.250 --> 00:25:00.490
So now that you
look at this series,

00:25:00.490 --> 00:25:03.080
I don't know, what do you think
are some reasonable values

00:25:03.080 --> 00:25:03.580
to encode?

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:11.555
Again, not a trick.

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:15.540
So this is just basics.

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:19.190
We are not even going to get
into hard technical problems

00:25:19.190 --> 00:25:19.690
yet.

00:25:25.250 --> 00:25:27.870
No one?

00:25:27.870 --> 00:25:29.370
Oh, OK.

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:31.620
I would have just
dropped all the decimals.

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:33.810
So it's something
temperature in Celsius.

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:38.610
I don't really care
if it's 38.1 or 37.9.

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:41.910
And if I were storing this data
for [INAUDIBLE]---- so again,

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:44.400
I said just some reasonable
values to encode,

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:45.870
being reasonably hand-wavy.

00:25:45.870 --> 00:25:48.150
You can come back and say,
no, I want another decimal.

00:25:48.150 --> 00:25:48.810
It's OK.

00:25:48.810 --> 00:25:52.912
But some reasonable values
could be just storing 38, 36,

00:25:52.912 --> 00:25:58.910
37, again 37, 35.

00:25:58.910 --> 00:25:59.900
Correct?

00:25:59.900 --> 00:26:04.440
And at this point, we have
already introduced loss.

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:08.400
So just rounding of
this data has led

00:26:08.400 --> 00:26:11.430
to an introduction of the loss.

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:13.440
And this is really important.

00:26:13.440 --> 00:26:15.120
And it's really
simple technique,

00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:19.590
but it's a first basic lossy
compression technique, which

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:21.930
is you can just round the data.

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:25.230
And that introduces loss.

00:26:25.230 --> 00:26:30.030
This is not exactly but similar
to converting the data types,

00:26:30.030 --> 00:26:35.040
so like converting the data
type from float to int.

00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:37.680
Maybe the sensor data in
practice you have been given

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:39.480
is actually, I don't
know, float 64;

00:26:39.480 --> 00:26:45.540
somebody is using float 64
to encode income of someone.

00:26:45.540 --> 00:26:47.033
Seems really wasteful, right?

00:26:47.033 --> 00:26:48.450
Like, as soon as
you get the data,

00:26:48.450 --> 00:26:52.470
the first thing you would do is
convert it into float 32 maybe,

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:54.210
or just some ints.

00:26:54.210 --> 00:26:57.150
At that point-- OK,
actually not ints.

00:26:57.150 --> 00:27:00.480
Maybe they care about--
people care about cents.

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:04.110
But as soon as you do
that, you have already

00:27:04.110 --> 00:27:05.550
introduced some loss.

00:27:05.550 --> 00:27:07.170
You have introduced
some distortion

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:09.570
into your source symbol.

00:27:09.570 --> 00:27:14.920
And that's really one
of the most, how should

00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:16.750
I say, obvious ways
in which we are doing

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:19.240
lossy compression going about.

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:20.680
And it's quite useful.

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:23.950
Just changing data types,
that's lossy compression.

00:27:23.950 --> 00:27:25.660
And it might lead
to much fewer bits

00:27:25.660 --> 00:27:29.215
to represent anything post that.

00:27:31.800 --> 00:27:35.070
OK, everybody with me so far?

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:38.940
The more formal way to think
about the obvious rounding

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:41.010
thing, which I said
and very obvious things

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:43.710
which we have been talking
about, is quantization.

00:27:43.710 --> 00:27:46.410
So what basically we did
in the previous example,

00:27:46.410 --> 00:27:49.200
it's called quantization.

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:51.690
So this is not the
dictionary definition.

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:55.200
I kind of got it
from Professor Gray

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:59.070
from Stanford, who was really
an expert in this area.

00:27:59.070 --> 00:28:00.990
In one of his notes,
he says that--

00:28:00.990 --> 00:28:02.910
and I really like this
simple definition,

00:28:02.910 --> 00:28:06.390
which is, quantization is
just the process of mapping

00:28:06.390 --> 00:28:09.140
a continuous source
to a discrete source.

00:28:09.140 --> 00:28:12.885
As soon as you do that, you have
moved into the lossy compression

00:28:12.885 --> 00:28:13.385
world.

00:28:15.890 --> 00:28:19.820
So some obvious points, like
quantization is a lossy process.

00:28:19.820 --> 00:28:22.430
It introduces distortion.

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:25.985
And in fact, it is a fundamental
operation in lossy compression.

00:28:29.220 --> 00:28:32.820
Yeah, I don't think any lossy
compressor in the world exists

00:28:32.820 --> 00:28:36.210
which doesn't do some form
of quantization somewhere,

00:28:36.210 --> 00:28:39.150
no matter what domain,
what application, what

00:28:39.150 --> 00:28:41.410
rate distortion level
you are talking about.

00:28:41.410 --> 00:28:42.840
So it is really like--

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:48.850
it's present somewhere as part
of the lossy compression scheme.

00:28:48.850 --> 00:28:52.360
So some technical terms--
the quantized values

00:28:52.360 --> 00:28:55.060
are called symbols-- sometimes
called symbols, sometimes

00:28:55.060 --> 00:28:57.100
called codewords.

00:28:57.100 --> 00:29:02.080
And the set of available
quantized values

00:29:02.080 --> 00:29:06.960
is sometimes called codebook,
sometimes called dictionary.

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:09.540
So in the previous example
which we looked at,

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:11.760
where we had 38.1
blah, blah, blah,

00:29:11.760 --> 00:29:14.130
let's say these were
the only five symbols.

00:29:14.130 --> 00:29:20.790
So I rounded it, and I
got 38, 36, 37, 37, 35.

00:29:20.790 --> 00:29:27.270
And so in this particular
case, my symbols or codewords

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:30.660
after quantization were just
these rounded-up things.

00:29:30.660 --> 00:29:33.210
And if you look at
this, it basically

00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:38.490
had only four unique
values, 35, 36, 37, 38.

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:40.740
And this is really
just your codebook.

00:29:40.740 --> 00:29:43.965
So these are the four quantized
values you would transmit.

00:29:47.430 --> 00:29:48.300
OK.

00:29:48.300 --> 00:29:52.140
So the next obvious
thing which we

00:29:52.140 --> 00:29:55.560
are going to ask-- so doing this
quantization reduce to some sort

00:29:55.560 --> 00:29:56.610
of [AUDIO OUT].

00:29:56.610 --> 00:29:58.410
We could think of
it in two ways.

00:29:58.410 --> 00:30:02.040
One, we could have thought of
it as, like I did, rounding.

00:30:02.040 --> 00:30:05.160
But another way to think
about this same thing

00:30:05.160 --> 00:30:09.640
is really that I
had this codebook.

00:30:09.640 --> 00:30:14.200
And then I wanted to represent
all these symbols using

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:15.220
the codebook.

00:30:15.220 --> 00:30:17.050
And so I will just
basically choose

00:30:17.050 --> 00:30:19.780
the element which is nearest
in the codebook, in some sense,

00:30:19.780 --> 00:30:23.770
being extremely hand-wavy will
get and fill in the details.

00:30:23.770 --> 00:30:26.328
But talking in English,
it makes sense, right?

00:30:26.328 --> 00:30:27.370
That's what you would do.

00:30:27.370 --> 00:30:30.700
These are the only four
values you can transmit ever.

00:30:30.700 --> 00:30:32.200
Then you would just
choose the value

00:30:32.200 --> 00:30:36.260
which is nearest to one
of these four values.

00:30:36.260 --> 00:30:39.320
So then let's say now I
have a codebook of size

00:30:39.320 --> 00:30:43.240
N. What is the rate going to be?

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:56.145
Again, no trick
questions, nothing wrong.

00:30:58.650 --> 00:31:00.480
Another way to think about it--

00:31:00.480 --> 00:31:04.590
think about-- OK, now we
are-- now we are in a world

00:31:04.590 --> 00:31:07.650
which you guys
understand, hopefully.

00:31:07.650 --> 00:31:09.570
These are discrete, right?

00:31:09.570 --> 00:31:11.580
Your codewords are discrete.

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:12.960
Let's say in this
case, there are

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:15.300
four elements, four codewords.

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:17.340
How would you transmit
these four codewords?

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:21.510
Let's say it's uniformly
distributed amongst these bits.

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:25.290
How many bits you
would have required?

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:26.970
I see a couple people pointing.

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:28.680
Two, right?

00:31:28.680 --> 00:31:29.730
Why two?

00:31:29.730 --> 00:31:31.320
What exactly is two?

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:33.880
Is it the log 2 of the
size of the codebook?

00:31:33.880 --> 00:31:34.690
Exactly.

00:31:34.690 --> 00:31:37.870
So it's just the log 2 of
the size of the codebook

00:31:37.870 --> 00:31:41.680
because what you can do
in this case is let's

00:31:41.680 --> 00:31:51.120
say I have codeword 1,
codeword 2, so on and so forth,

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:54.420
codeword n.

00:31:54.420 --> 00:31:58.320
And if I have to transmit
just the codewords,

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:03.430
I could encode it by
just encoding the index.

00:32:03.430 --> 00:32:06.880
And then if I only have to send
the index-- so for example,

00:32:06.880 --> 00:32:12.880
let's say if I receive index i,
I will reconstruct codeword i.

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:16.390
My decoder will
reconstruct codeword i.

00:32:16.390 --> 00:32:19.870
So in this case, you only
need to send log N bits.

00:32:23.550 --> 00:32:27.000
So if you have N
codewords, you only

00:32:27.000 --> 00:32:29.250
need to send
actually log N bits.

00:32:33.040 --> 00:32:35.620
And so if you have a
codebook of size N,

00:32:35.620 --> 00:32:37.930
irrespective of what
exactly the code vector

00:32:37.930 --> 00:32:49.540
is, your rate is just going to
be log 2 of N. So again, sorry,

00:32:49.540 --> 00:32:52.970
there should be a ceil here.

00:32:52.970 --> 00:32:54.350
Let me just add it now.

00:33:00.730 --> 00:33:06.940
Another way to think about this
is if you had bits per symbol,

00:33:06.940 --> 00:33:10.510
you could have really used
2 to power R unique values.

00:33:10.510 --> 00:33:12.670
And this is the
exact same principle

00:33:12.670 --> 00:33:14.410
if we go back to,
I think, lecture

00:33:14.410 --> 00:33:18.110
5 about asymptotic
equipartition property which

00:33:18.110 --> 00:33:19.682
Tsachy talked about.

00:33:19.682 --> 00:33:20.890
This is the exact same thing.

00:33:20.890 --> 00:33:24.460
You can just--
you can just count

00:33:24.460 --> 00:33:26.980
the indices and the
sequence and use that

00:33:26.980 --> 00:33:28.165
to just encode the index.

00:33:30.850 --> 00:33:34.930
So this tells if you have
a codebook of size N,

00:33:34.930 --> 00:33:41.290
you need roughly log N bits
per symbol to transmit it.

00:33:41.290 --> 00:33:44.800
And this tells you about
the rate distortion tradeoff

00:33:44.800 --> 00:33:46.570
now immediately.

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:50.950
So let's say you had an
opportunity for higher rate.

00:33:50.950 --> 00:33:57.750
In that case, you would
have a larger codebook.

00:33:57.750 --> 00:34:00.660
A larger codebook would
result in a lower distortion

00:34:00.660 --> 00:34:04.840
because now you have more
options to choose from.

00:34:04.840 --> 00:34:07.690
Alternate way to think about
the same thing, if you had,

00:34:07.690 --> 00:34:10.610
I don't know, a lower
codebook, in that case,

00:34:10.610 --> 00:34:13.960
you would probably have
a higher distortion.

00:34:13.960 --> 00:34:15.804
And then you would
need fewer bits.

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:22.500
Maybe let's just
quickly write it down.

00:34:22.500 --> 00:34:27.179
So if your rate is
higher, that basically

00:34:27.179 --> 00:34:30.179
means your number of
codewords is higher.

00:34:30.179 --> 00:34:35.630
And that would typically means
your distortion goes down.

00:34:35.630 --> 00:34:38.330
Or other way if we have
to think, if you're--

00:34:38.330 --> 00:34:41.989
I don't know, if you're
OK with higher distortion,

00:34:41.989 --> 00:34:46.520
you would do so by reducing
your codebook size.

00:34:46.520 --> 00:34:51.110
And that would lead
to a lower rate.

00:34:51.110 --> 00:34:54.560
So you see the tradeoff
between rate distortion

00:34:54.560 --> 00:34:55.660
immediately in the scheme.

00:35:00.320 --> 00:35:01.535
Any questions so far?

00:35:07.670 --> 00:35:12.110
OK, so now let's talk
about another example.

00:35:12.110 --> 00:35:15.260
Like we have been saying,
the lossy compression

00:35:15.260 --> 00:35:17.240
makes sense and
continuous sources.

00:35:17.240 --> 00:35:19.520
And whenever sources
come in life,

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:22.730
the first thought
for everyone should--

00:35:22.730 --> 00:35:25.010
like modeling continuous
sources comes in,

00:35:25.010 --> 00:35:29.480
the first thought should go
to a Gaussian random variable.

00:35:29.480 --> 00:35:34.240
Is everyone comfortable with
Gaussian random variable?

00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:35.200
Yeah?

00:35:35.200 --> 00:35:35.710
Good.

00:35:35.710 --> 00:35:40.450
OK, so now let's say
you have symbol X--

00:35:40.450 --> 00:35:43.330
set of symbols X, which are
coming from a Gaussian source.

00:35:43.330 --> 00:35:45.505
And let's just say it
has mean 0 variance 1.

00:35:48.610 --> 00:35:52.180
So it's a continuous source.

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:55.690
And for whatever reason,
your friend comes and says,

00:35:55.690 --> 00:35:59.020
oh, I can only send
like 1 bit per symbol.

00:35:59.020 --> 00:36:00.320
I just can't do more than that.

00:36:00.320 --> 00:36:01.300
That's my budget.

00:36:01.300 --> 00:36:05.877
That's the-- I'm in, I don't
know, 1930s or, whatever, 1960s.

00:36:05.877 --> 00:36:07.960
There wasn't [INAUDIBLE]
then, and I can only send

00:36:07.960 --> 00:36:10.720
a bit per second or something.

00:36:10.720 --> 00:36:14.450
But this is my source.

00:36:14.450 --> 00:36:16.190
Now, what do you think?

00:36:16.190 --> 00:36:19.115
What's some reasonable values
to encode for this source?

00:36:25.010 --> 00:36:25.850
The sign.

00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:27.440
The sign, OK, yeah.

00:36:27.440 --> 00:36:28.580
That makes sense.

00:36:28.580 --> 00:36:31.190
So one of the students
suggested, OK, maybe we

00:36:31.190 --> 00:36:32.750
should encode the sign.

00:36:32.750 --> 00:36:34.880
And that basically just
comes from the fact

00:36:34.880 --> 00:36:39.020
that Gaussian really
is like a symmetrical

00:36:39.020 --> 00:36:43.230
distribution in terms
of probability density.

00:36:43.230 --> 00:36:44.910
And so if you have
only one bit that

00:36:44.910 --> 00:36:49.650
says you can only send some two
choices, maybe sending the sign

00:36:49.650 --> 00:36:51.360
makes intuitive sense.

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:56.835
OK, so again, we are not saying
so far if it's the best--

00:36:56.835 --> 00:36:59.220
we can't say if
it is the best way

00:36:59.220 --> 00:37:01.410
to do until you come
and tell me what's

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:06.190
the distortion I'm
really OK with.

00:37:06.190 --> 00:37:08.980
But it still makes
intuitive sense that,

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:11.560
OK, hopefully whatever
distortion someone comes up

00:37:11.560 --> 00:37:13.240
with will be symmetric around 0.

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:15.040
And otherwise, they probably--

00:37:15.040 --> 00:37:17.980
something is wrong or
Gaussian model is wrong right.

00:37:17.980 --> 00:37:18.910
But it's OK.

00:37:18.910 --> 00:37:24.820
OK, so one reasonable valuable
value to encode is sign of X,

00:37:24.820 --> 00:37:27.040
and that's just because
your distribution

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:28.630
is symmetric around 0.

00:37:31.420 --> 00:37:32.290
Everyone with me?

00:37:36.700 --> 00:37:39.730
OK, so let's continue.

00:37:39.730 --> 00:37:43.930
So your friend says you can just
transmit one bit per symbol.

00:37:43.930 --> 00:37:48.460
And so you say, OK, I'll
encode the sign of X hat.

00:37:48.460 --> 00:37:50.380
Sorry, I'll just
encode the sign of X.

00:37:50.380 --> 00:37:54.460
So no matter what my X is,
at each of these symbols,

00:37:54.460 --> 00:37:57.550
I'll just send you the sign.

00:37:57.550 --> 00:38:00.160
So now let's say
your friend receives

00:38:00.160 --> 00:38:05.110
a positive value for X hat,
what should he really recover?

00:38:05.110 --> 00:38:10.400
What should he-- what should
he represent that symbol with?

00:38:10.400 --> 00:38:12.950
So other words, what should
be the quantized value

00:38:12.950 --> 00:38:15.800
of the recovered symbol?

00:38:15.800 --> 00:38:18.950
To remind, like it was, for
example, last time from 37.1,

00:38:18.950 --> 00:38:20.450
we went to 37, right?

00:38:20.450 --> 00:38:22.040
So that's the quantized value.

00:38:25.620 --> 00:38:27.390
And to actually
answer this question,

00:38:27.390 --> 00:38:29.490
this is what I was just
saying a second ago,

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:32.730
you need to decide what
distortion are we OK with.

00:38:32.730 --> 00:38:36.990
So different distortions might
mean a differently colored

00:38:36.990 --> 00:38:39.030
symbol may make better sense.

00:38:41.747 --> 00:38:44.080
OK, so let's say we are talking
about mean squared error

00:38:44.080 --> 00:38:47.460
distortion, one
specific distortion.

00:38:47.460 --> 00:38:49.560
Any guesses?

00:38:49.560 --> 00:38:50.850
OK, so this one is technical.

00:38:50.850 --> 00:38:52.875
This one is not a joke question.

00:38:55.500 --> 00:38:57.810
The variance.

00:38:57.810 --> 00:38:58.770
The variance?

00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:01.920
Why do you say so?

00:39:01.920 --> 00:39:07.560
The variance is the expectation
of the MSE [INAUDIBLE]..

00:39:07.560 --> 00:39:13.940
OK, actually, no,
that's incorrect.

00:39:13.940 --> 00:39:18.260
So the answer really is
the expected value of X

00:39:18.260 --> 00:39:22.430
given your observed symbol,
which is X hat greater than 0.

00:39:22.430 --> 00:39:28.616
And your obvious question should
be, where does this come from?

00:39:28.616 --> 00:39:30.830
This is a really celebrated--

00:39:30.830 --> 00:39:32.510
do I have links?

00:39:32.510 --> 00:39:35.000
Actually, I don't have links
on these slides, but it's OK.

00:39:35.000 --> 00:39:38.090
So this is a really
celebrated result,

00:39:38.090 --> 00:39:42.110
and it's called minimum
mean square estimator.

00:39:42.110 --> 00:39:51.190
So minimum mean
square estimator.

00:39:51.190 --> 00:39:55.480
And if you have taken, I
guess, a signal processing

00:39:55.480 --> 00:39:59.320
class for mean squared error,
this should be obvious for you.

00:39:59.320 --> 00:40:03.670
But it's OK if you don't know
this result. For this class

00:40:03.670 --> 00:40:07.030
purposes, you can assume
that this is true.

00:40:07.030 --> 00:40:11.200
What this means is that
if somebody comes and says

00:40:11.200 --> 00:40:17.560
to me that I have X
hat greater than 0,

00:40:17.560 --> 00:40:28.250
the value which minimizes this
thing, given X hat greater

00:40:28.250 --> 00:40:31.580
than 0, is basically
expected value of X

00:40:31.580 --> 00:40:33.455
given X hat greater than 0.

00:40:41.100 --> 00:40:43.740
There are more
references in the notes,

00:40:43.740 --> 00:40:51.620
which should be pushed by now
on the class notes website.

00:40:51.620 --> 00:40:54.060
So if you are eager, you
can go look into this.

00:40:54.060 --> 00:40:56.600
There are some signal
processing references there also

00:40:56.600 --> 00:40:59.060
which you can go through.

00:40:59.060 --> 00:41:01.730
But yeah, so this is really--

00:41:01.730 --> 00:41:03.445
this is a really
fundamental result.

00:41:03.445 --> 00:41:04.820
A lot of people
lossy compression

00:41:04.820 --> 00:41:06.770
will just replace it.

00:41:06.770 --> 00:41:08.720
But it's really
important to understand

00:41:08.720 --> 00:41:15.090
that this particular codebook
is for mean square estimator.

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:17.730
So this is like a minimum
mean square estimator.

00:41:17.730 --> 00:41:20.342
If I came up with some
crazy distortion function--

00:41:20.342 --> 00:41:22.050
I don't know, I could
have come up with--

00:41:25.470 --> 00:41:26.925
I don't-- let's say--

00:41:38.930 --> 00:41:42.380
I don't know, let's say I came
up with a distortion function

00:41:42.380 --> 00:41:42.935
like this.

00:41:45.470 --> 00:41:46.570
It may not be true.

00:41:46.570 --> 00:41:50.740
Or some more nonlinear, like
a neural net X minus X hat

00:41:50.740 --> 00:41:51.460
something--

00:41:54.370 --> 00:41:57.400
so the point which I
really want to make here

00:41:57.400 --> 00:42:06.950
is that this optimal codebook,
once you have the rate,

00:42:06.950 --> 00:42:10.820
so you basically
know your codebook,

00:42:10.820 --> 00:42:13.580
and you really need to know
the distortion for which you

00:42:13.580 --> 00:42:14.330
are optimizing.

00:42:14.330 --> 00:42:16.393
Otherwise, there is
no optimal codebook

00:42:16.393 --> 00:42:18.560
you need to be always--
like whenever somebody comes

00:42:18.560 --> 00:42:20.870
and talks to you-- talks to
you about lossy, ask them

00:42:20.870 --> 00:42:23.480
what's the distortion
we are talking about.

00:42:23.480 --> 00:42:27.800
Or you might have to define
the appropriate distortion.

00:42:27.800 --> 00:42:29.750
And actually, if you
work this out-- again,

00:42:29.750 --> 00:42:33.440
this is there in the notes.

00:42:33.440 --> 00:42:38.420
So this-- or we can just
quickly work it out here also

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:41.870
just so that people
feel comfortable.

00:42:41.870 --> 00:42:48.230
So expected value of X
given X hat greater than 0.

00:42:48.230 --> 00:42:54.320
So we know fX is Gaussian, which
just means it's density is this.

00:42:57.130 --> 00:43:03.580
And expected value of X
given X hat equals to 0, just

00:43:03.580 --> 00:43:06.880
by the definition,
would be minus infinity

00:43:06.880 --> 00:43:15.940
to infinity X times fX given
X hat greater than 0 of X dX.

00:43:15.940 --> 00:43:18.220
And this is just a
conditional distribution,

00:43:18.220 --> 00:43:22.150
so you can use Bayes
rule to recover this.

00:43:22.150 --> 00:43:24.730
In this case, it's symmetric.

00:43:24.730 --> 00:43:27.340
So f of X, given X
hat greater than 0

00:43:27.340 --> 00:43:31.750
of X. Since it's
symmetric around 0,

00:43:31.750 --> 00:43:33.940
it will just be twice
this distribution,

00:43:33.940 --> 00:43:44.300
so it will be 2 times f of X
given X. You can show this.

00:43:44.300 --> 00:43:47.465
This is just straightforward--

00:43:47.465 --> 00:43:52.390
This is straightforward maths.

00:43:52.390 --> 00:43:57.700
And so if you write this
down, this basically becomes,

00:43:57.700 --> 00:44:01.435
let's say, square
root 2 over pi times X

00:44:01.435 --> 00:44:04.240
e raised to power
minus X pi 2 dX.

00:44:04.240 --> 00:44:07.540
And actually, it's X
hat greater than 0.

00:44:07.540 --> 00:44:09.700
So this is this.

00:44:09.700 --> 00:44:15.900
If X is greater than 0, 0
else because N equals to 0.

00:44:15.900 --> 00:44:18.810
So this is also called--

00:44:18.810 --> 00:44:21.570
this is just half Gaussian,
like it's just looking

00:44:21.570 --> 00:44:23.220
at one side of the Gaussian.

00:44:23.220 --> 00:44:26.100
And if you work
out this integral,

00:44:26.100 --> 00:44:29.310
it's actually quite
straightforward,

00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:34.080
like you could do a variable
substitution here of X square

00:44:34.080 --> 00:44:34.670
by 2.

00:44:34.670 --> 00:44:36.560
OK, this is not
a calculus class.

00:44:36.560 --> 00:44:37.560
So we can skip this you.

00:44:37.560 --> 00:44:39.810
Can trust me on this part.

00:44:39.810 --> 00:44:42.420
This thing will basically--
and again, the notes

00:44:42.420 --> 00:44:43.170
have more details.

00:44:43.170 --> 00:44:46.830
So if you want to look into,
you can-- you're happy to look.

00:44:46.830 --> 00:44:48.180
Please check it out.

00:44:48.180 --> 00:44:53.920
So this gives you square root
2 over pi, which is this.

00:44:53.920 --> 00:44:58.270
And then expected value of X,
again, given X hat less than 0

00:44:58.270 --> 00:44:59.020
is just symmetric.

00:44:59.020 --> 00:45:00.835
So it's just negative
value of that.

00:45:04.370 --> 00:45:06.840
So in this example--

00:45:06.840 --> 00:45:13.950
so this match was just to do
it once in front of you guys

00:45:13.950 --> 00:45:18.000
so that you feel comfortable
with what's happening.

00:45:18.000 --> 00:45:24.000
It's not really required from
the purpose of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:45:24.000 --> 00:45:27.870
We'll not assume you know
signal processing a lot,

00:45:27.870 --> 00:45:29.700
so don't worry about
if you are seeing

00:45:29.700 --> 00:45:32.490
this for the first time in
your life and are like, OK.

00:45:32.490 --> 00:45:34.590
But this is really one of the--

00:45:34.590 --> 00:45:37.410
I don't know, it's one of
the most celebrated results

00:45:37.410 --> 00:45:40.275
in signal processing, used
hundreds of different of places.

00:45:43.200 --> 00:45:44.280
Main takeaway really is--

00:45:44.280 --> 00:45:47.100
OK, so if somebody gave
you a Gaussian and said,

00:45:47.100 --> 00:45:50.460
OK, one bit per symbol,
that basically implies you

00:45:50.460 --> 00:45:52.920
have a codebook which can
only contain two symbols.

00:45:52.920 --> 00:45:55.110
And one of the choices
for mean square,

00:45:55.110 --> 00:45:57.840
estimator like if you
were minimizing distortion

00:45:57.840 --> 00:46:04.300
for mean square estimator, is
you would choose these values.

00:46:04.300 --> 00:46:08.050
So again, the communication
scheme, more importantly,

00:46:08.050 --> 00:46:10.900
looks like this.

00:46:10.900 --> 00:46:13.720
Oops, what did I do?

00:46:13.720 --> 00:46:20.910
So let's just quickly talk about
the sort of scheme which we did.

00:46:20.910 --> 00:46:21.840
What did we do?

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:26.280
We started with a
continuous source.

00:46:26.280 --> 00:46:29.640
We decided that we want to
encode it at one bit per symbol.

00:46:32.550 --> 00:46:36.030
Actually, let me not even put
arrows because some of these

00:46:36.030 --> 00:46:36.940
are decisions.

00:46:40.320 --> 00:46:45.720
We thought that encoding sign
seems a reasonable thing to do.

00:46:45.720 --> 00:46:49.110
So what you did was you
communicated and encoded just

00:46:49.110 --> 00:46:54.070
the sign, so this
thing you encoded,

00:46:54.070 --> 00:46:58.390
which is a discrete distribution
so you can see this step here,

00:46:58.390 --> 00:47:00.370
encoding a discrete
distribution,

00:47:00.370 --> 00:47:04.720
is where everything we learnt
in lossless will come into play.

00:47:04.720 --> 00:47:07.090
It doesn't right now,
but you can already

00:47:07.090 --> 00:47:09.310
see what's going to happen.

00:47:09.310 --> 00:47:10.930
So you encode the sign.

00:47:10.930 --> 00:47:19.430
You send over index
of the codebook.

00:47:19.430 --> 00:47:24.630
Or send over as just encoded
the index of the codebook.

00:47:24.630 --> 00:47:26.910
On the other side,
your decoder receives

00:47:26.910 --> 00:47:33.120
this, this exact same thing,
index of the codebook,

00:47:33.120 --> 00:47:35.370
and use it to recover the sign.

00:47:35.370 --> 00:47:36.720
So they know the sign.

00:47:36.720 --> 00:47:38.700
But actually, not just
the sign, since you

00:47:38.700 --> 00:47:41.340
guys agreed on mean
square estimation.

00:47:41.340 --> 00:47:43.230
So you already had
the codeword shared,

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:46.980
codebook either shared or
can be computed independently

00:47:46.980 --> 00:47:48.240
at the decoder side.

00:47:48.240 --> 00:47:50.580
And that basically
told them, OK, I'll

00:47:50.580 --> 00:47:53.880
just represent my
thing as given the sign

00:47:53.880 --> 00:47:55.830
as plus minus
square root 2 by pi.

00:47:55.830 --> 00:48:00.810
And that would minimize my
overall mean square distortion.

00:48:00.810 --> 00:48:02.700
This is really the
scheme which we did.

00:48:10.460 --> 00:48:13.430
OK, everyone with me?

00:48:13.430 --> 00:48:17.620
At least schematically
we understand

00:48:17.620 --> 00:48:19.990
what's happening in
both these examples

00:48:19.990 --> 00:48:22.120
and how are we introducing loss?

00:48:22.120 --> 00:48:25.030
What are the key
things to look out for?

00:48:25.030 --> 00:48:28.000
We'll keep on making it more
and more formal as we go ahead.

00:48:32.640 --> 00:48:34.270
Cool.

00:48:34.270 --> 00:48:36.570
So what we did was--

00:48:36.570 --> 00:48:38.820
so this is the
Gaussian histogram.

00:48:38.820 --> 00:48:42.630
So same Gaussian, normal 0, 1.

00:48:42.630 --> 00:48:44.430
I generated a lot of samples.

00:48:44.430 --> 00:48:46.410
I just plotted it
as a histogram.

00:48:46.410 --> 00:48:50.340
This is X. These are the counts.

00:48:50.340 --> 00:48:52.620
So this is not the
probability, but you

00:48:52.620 --> 00:48:55.140
can see different counts.

00:48:55.140 --> 00:48:56.775
And this is roughly my Gaussian.

00:48:59.440 --> 00:49:01.570
So in this whole
scheme, you basically

00:49:01.570 --> 00:49:04.930
did two things,
which is how we'll

00:49:04.930 --> 00:49:07.960
go ahead and formalize this.

00:49:07.960 --> 00:49:12.970
You decided a decision boundary
for one bit per symbol.

00:49:12.970 --> 00:49:16.990
So one of your decision
boundary was X greater than 0.

00:49:16.990 --> 00:49:22.020
The other decision boundary was,
I don't know, X less than 0,

00:49:22.020 --> 00:49:23.010
correct?

00:49:23.010 --> 00:49:25.230
And then you went
ahead and mapped it

00:49:25.230 --> 00:49:27.840
to a specific value
of the codebook.

00:49:32.490 --> 00:49:35.040
And so, let's say, if
you have a point here--

00:49:35.040 --> 00:49:37.590
like if your actual
sample was, I don't know,

00:49:37.590 --> 00:49:40.140
2 point something, you
would basically just

00:49:40.140 --> 00:49:44.490
map it to the-- so this value
is square root 2 over pi.

00:49:44.490 --> 00:49:47.500
You'll just map it to this guy.

00:49:47.500 --> 00:49:49.420
Or if there was a point here--

00:49:49.420 --> 00:49:52.360
you'll basically see which
point of the decision boundary

00:49:52.360 --> 00:49:55.300
your actual symbol
lies 2 and just

00:49:55.300 --> 00:49:58.240
map it to the corresponding
codebook word.

00:50:05.070 --> 00:50:08.880
And so more formally, like
exactly the whole thing

00:50:08.880 --> 00:50:11.280
which we did, is an
example of something

00:50:11.280 --> 00:50:14.430
called scalar quantization.

00:50:14.430 --> 00:50:16.470
So the idea of scalar
is you are just

00:50:16.470 --> 00:50:18.540
looking at each
individual symbol,

00:50:18.540 --> 00:50:20.790
and you are defining two things.

00:50:20.790 --> 00:50:23.460
One, so now here
what I'm showing

00:50:23.460 --> 00:50:26.850
is input signal X, so another
way to show the same thing,

00:50:26.850 --> 00:50:29.940
and the output X hat.

00:50:29.940 --> 00:50:32.640
Your quantizer
really is a function.

00:50:32.640 --> 00:50:38.990
It takes an input X and maps
it to a quantized value X hat.

00:50:38.990 --> 00:50:44.540
OK, so you can see this
function as a 2D xy plot, right?

00:50:44.540 --> 00:50:46.310
And the two things
which we looked at

00:50:46.310 --> 00:50:47.990
was the decision thresholds.

00:50:47.990 --> 00:50:50.300
So right now, we only
talked about one bit,

00:50:50.300 --> 00:50:52.085
but obviously we
could have decided

00:50:52.085 --> 00:50:53.210
we could have had two bits.

00:50:53.210 --> 00:50:54.470
We could have had N bits.

00:50:54.470 --> 00:50:58.020
We could have various different
number of bits, right?

00:50:58.020 --> 00:51:00.450
And so a scalar
quantizer is basically

00:51:00.450 --> 00:51:03.120
being defined by the
decision thresholds.

00:51:03.120 --> 00:51:06.750
So you are selecting
the regions which

00:51:06.750 --> 00:51:09.300
will be mapped to a
single value and then

00:51:09.300 --> 00:51:12.520
the corresponding
quantized values.

00:51:12.520 --> 00:51:16.930
Again, in the previous
example, my decision regions

00:51:16.930 --> 00:51:18.820
were X greater than
0, X less than 0.

00:51:18.820 --> 00:51:20.860
So my decision threshold was 0.

00:51:20.860 --> 00:51:25.300
And my quantize values were plus
minus square root 2 over pi.

00:51:25.300 --> 00:51:29.110
But more generally,
I can select--

00:51:29.110 --> 00:51:31.750
given any bit rate, I
can select the codebook,

00:51:31.750 --> 00:51:34.270
so I know exactly
the quantized values

00:51:34.270 --> 00:51:36.580
which are going to be
part of that codebook.

00:51:36.580 --> 00:51:39.430
And then I can select
these decision thresholds.

00:51:44.940 --> 00:51:49.720
Again, like here, this
particular quantized value

00:51:49.720 --> 00:51:51.610
was determined
based on optimizing

00:51:51.610 --> 00:51:54.370
MSC, where we did expected
value-- we calculated

00:51:54.370 --> 00:51:56.540
this expected value.

00:51:56.540 --> 00:51:58.370
But I could have chosen this.

00:51:58.370 --> 00:52:01.820
I could have chosen
something else.

00:52:01.820 --> 00:52:05.960
So these two elements completely
determine a scalar quantizer.

00:52:11.610 --> 00:52:13.710
And then, OK, so I
don't have it here,

00:52:13.710 --> 00:52:18.150
but something which you see
is your decision regions

00:52:18.150 --> 00:52:20.520
need not be uniform.

00:52:20.520 --> 00:52:22.590
Nobody is saying that
they need to be uniform.

00:52:22.590 --> 00:52:27.630
So basically what I mean is
each of these widths on x,

00:52:27.630 --> 00:52:28.560
they can be different.

00:52:28.560 --> 00:52:30.690
That's still a scalar quantizer.

00:52:30.690 --> 00:52:33.390
You can decide what
these needs to be.

00:52:33.390 --> 00:52:36.540
And that can be determined based
on whatever your distortion

00:52:36.540 --> 00:52:37.425
function really is.

00:52:41.990 --> 00:52:42.650
OK, great.

00:52:42.650 --> 00:52:43.970
Everyone with me?

00:52:49.210 --> 00:52:50.035
So then--

00:52:56.797 --> 00:52:57.880
We looked at quantization.

00:52:57.880 --> 00:52:59.020
We understand this method.

00:52:59.020 --> 00:52:59.860
Seems very simple.

00:53:02.430 --> 00:53:07.680
Any suggestions on what more we
can do with this quantization?

00:53:07.680 --> 00:53:10.830
Again, looking back at maybe
what we did in lossless

00:53:10.830 --> 00:53:11.700
compression.

00:53:14.680 --> 00:53:18.730
So right now, remember,
a scheme right now--

00:53:18.730 --> 00:53:24.145
sorry, that page somehow
vanished, which I just wrote.

00:53:28.790 --> 00:53:31.040
Yeah, OK, so what did we do?

00:53:31.040 --> 00:53:33.560
We took one symbol at a time.

00:53:33.560 --> 00:53:37.460
We decide where it lies in
the corresponding decision

00:53:37.460 --> 00:53:38.150
threshold.

00:53:38.150 --> 00:53:41.420
And then we map it to some
quantized value X hat.

00:53:41.420 --> 00:53:43.970
We encode it, transmit
it, do whatever.

00:53:47.230 --> 00:53:49.450
Can we do something--

00:53:49.450 --> 00:53:50.800
any other ideas, actually?

00:53:50.800 --> 00:53:56.537
Again, you can think about what
we think we did in lossless.

00:53:56.537 --> 00:53:58.120
Based on our previous
symbol, we could

00:53:58.120 --> 00:54:00.490
adjust the decision thresholds.

00:54:00.490 --> 00:54:03.510
OK, so one idea
which I got was--

00:54:03.510 --> 00:54:05.770
one student said based
on the previous symbol,

00:54:05.770 --> 00:54:07.660
we can adjust the threshold.

00:54:07.660 --> 00:54:09.790
That's actually a
very valid idea.

00:54:09.790 --> 00:54:13.492
People do that, but that's
one step even more ahead.

00:54:13.492 --> 00:54:15.700
We are not going to talk
about this in today's class.

00:54:15.700 --> 00:54:19.310
But again, I have some added
some references in the notes.

00:54:19.310 --> 00:54:21.880
So if you want to look into
that, you can look into that.

00:54:21.880 --> 00:54:25.030
We'll see some examples of
that when we learn about,

00:54:25.030 --> 00:54:28.810
I think, image codecs,
like BPG, et cetera.

00:54:28.810 --> 00:54:30.910
But that's a
perfectly valid idea.

00:54:30.910 --> 00:54:35.560
Even before that, think of this
as a parallel to lecture 1 or 2,

00:54:35.560 --> 00:54:37.090
when we started with lossless.

00:54:46.970 --> 00:54:50.120
What was one thing which always
gave you a better compression

00:54:50.120 --> 00:54:51.790
if you didn't care
about complexity?

00:54:55.985 --> 00:54:56.485
Sorry?

00:54:59.530 --> 00:55:03.220
Yes, so the answer is-- yeah,
it's like combining two blocks.

00:55:03.220 --> 00:55:06.190
So that was one idea which
seemed to have worked really

00:55:06.190 --> 00:55:09.010
well for lossless compression.

00:55:09.010 --> 00:55:11.830
Question is, can we use it--

00:55:11.830 --> 00:55:13.180
does it work well?

00:55:13.180 --> 00:55:15.370
Or how does it do for
the lossy compression?

00:55:15.370 --> 00:55:16.947
Maybe combining
symbols together,

00:55:16.947 --> 00:55:19.030
we could have done better
instead of just deciding

00:55:19.030 --> 00:55:22.030
based on a single symbol.

00:55:22.030 --> 00:55:27.080
And that's exactly the idea
of vector quantization.

00:55:27.080 --> 00:55:32.570
So scalar to vector, vector kind
of refers to, in some sense,

00:55:32.570 --> 00:55:34.790
the symbols in a block.

00:55:34.790 --> 00:55:37.985
[INAUDIBLE] idea is exactly--

00:55:37.985 --> 00:55:40.610
I have written the same like how
the student put it down maybe.

00:55:40.610 --> 00:55:43.220
Maybe we can work with two
symbols at a time or three

00:55:43.220 --> 00:55:45.830
or N symbols at a time.

00:55:45.830 --> 00:55:47.195
Can it do something better?

00:55:49.840 --> 00:55:51.820
To start with the
discussion, let's say

00:55:51.820 --> 00:55:54.880
we are working with
just two symbols.

00:55:54.880 --> 00:55:59.947
So then you can think of your
X as blocks of two, right?

00:55:59.947 --> 00:56:02.530
So you are just-- so there are
many different ways of thinking

00:56:02.530 --> 00:56:05.020
of vector quantization.

00:56:05.020 --> 00:56:08.290
So you can think of it as like
you generated two N symbols

00:56:08.290 --> 00:56:12.190
and converted into two
groups of sizes N each.

00:56:12.190 --> 00:56:13.840
So there's like
similar block codes.

00:56:13.840 --> 00:56:16.150
I'll just take the two symbol,
two symbol, two symbol,

00:56:16.150 --> 00:56:19.990
two symbol, and then I'll try
to quantize this vector instead

00:56:19.990 --> 00:56:23.610
of quantizing just the scalar.

00:56:23.610 --> 00:56:24.990
[INAUDIBLE]

00:56:26.610 --> 00:56:28.290
It could also have been--

00:56:28.290 --> 00:56:30.180
instead of block
codes, [INAUDIBLE]

00:56:30.180 --> 00:56:31.110
is a bit [INAUDIBLE].

00:56:31.110 --> 00:56:34.050
Instead of block, you
could have thought of it

00:56:34.050 --> 00:56:37.320
as two sensors which are
measuring the same source.

00:56:37.320 --> 00:56:39.420
So you had-- so
one way is you can

00:56:39.420 --> 00:56:41.310
think you had a single
temperature sensor,

00:56:41.310 --> 00:56:43.590
and then you were
clubbing every two symbol,

00:56:43.590 --> 00:56:46.320
and that's a vector of size two,
and then you will do something

00:56:46.320 --> 00:56:47.190
on top of it.

00:56:47.190 --> 00:56:49.530
But maybe it was just
two different sensors,

00:56:49.530 --> 00:56:51.270
two different time series.

00:56:51.270 --> 00:56:58.340
And then that can be represented
[AUDIO OUT] individually.

00:56:58.340 --> 00:57:01.070
Or you could have even had two
different sensors just measuring

00:57:01.070 --> 00:57:03.432
two different sources.

00:57:03.432 --> 00:57:05.390
You could have one sensor
measuring temperature

00:57:05.390 --> 00:57:09.580
of one room, another
measuring of another room.

00:57:09.580 --> 00:57:11.380
So vector is just like--

00:57:11.380 --> 00:57:13.663
you could have combined
any two tuples,

00:57:13.663 --> 00:57:15.080
and they would
have made a vector.

00:57:15.080 --> 00:57:16.990
So it's a bit more general.

00:57:16.990 --> 00:57:20.740
But the basic way to
think about it really is--

00:57:20.740 --> 00:57:23.545
as a student also pointed out,
it's basically block codes.

00:57:28.160 --> 00:57:33.320
OK, so now let's say
I have specific case.

00:57:33.320 --> 00:57:34.720
I have a block size of two.

00:57:38.020 --> 00:57:41.120
And I want to design
a vector [AUDIO OUT]

00:57:41.120 --> 00:57:43.220
the reasons and
decision thresholds.

00:57:43.220 --> 00:57:46.730
Instead of along a line,
like we did in the Gaussian,

00:57:46.730 --> 00:57:50.680
we'll do it over a 2D plane, and
I'll show you some [AUDIO OUT]

00:57:50.680 --> 00:57:51.180
do it.

00:58:00.680 --> 00:58:04.730
So I want to compare it,
like whether it helps or not.

00:58:04.730 --> 00:58:07.490
I want to compare it with
the one bit per symbol scalar

00:58:07.490 --> 00:58:08.420
quantizer.

00:58:11.050 --> 00:58:15.530
What is the size of
codebook allowed now?

00:58:15.530 --> 00:58:17.300
So again, remember this game.

00:58:17.300 --> 00:58:22.960
Now I'll map each X1 X2--

00:58:22.960 --> 00:58:24.550
let me actually
just write it down.

00:58:24.550 --> 00:58:26.590
So you'll have a
codebook, which is,

00:58:26.590 --> 00:58:30.100
let's say, indexed by 1,
2, N, some size codebook,

00:58:30.100 --> 00:58:33.070
code vectors.

00:58:33.070 --> 00:58:37.660
You get a tuple,
let's say, X1 X2.

00:58:37.660 --> 00:58:40.480
And you want to find--

00:58:40.480 --> 00:58:43.780
you'll map it to one
of these indices.

00:58:43.780 --> 00:58:46.840
And then encode
that index, right?

00:58:46.840 --> 00:58:51.010
So what can be the size of
your codebook if you were--

00:58:51.010 --> 00:58:53.710
if your rate was
1 bit per symbol

00:58:53.710 --> 00:58:56.440
IF that's what you
want to work with?

00:59:01.460 --> 00:59:02.270
4.

00:59:02.270 --> 00:59:04.580
OK, 4 is the answer
by this student.

00:59:04.580 --> 00:59:05.804
Why 4?

00:59:05.804 --> 00:59:08.174
Because you have 2
symbols equals 2 bits

00:59:08.174 --> 00:59:10.550
so you'll have 4 symbols.

00:59:10.550 --> 00:59:11.210
Exactly.

00:59:11.210 --> 00:59:14.360
So the student said
we have 2 symbols.

00:59:14.360 --> 00:59:16.430
So basically 1 bit
per symbol-- so this

00:59:16.430 --> 00:59:21.050
is exact same logic as we worked
through during the block codes.

00:59:21.050 --> 00:59:26.470
This allows for 2 bit
per block, correct?

00:59:26.470 --> 00:59:29.380
And then this basically
allows-- so as I said,

00:59:29.380 --> 00:59:32.980
we are encoding block as
one element of the codebook.

00:59:32.980 --> 00:59:37.810
So this basically says I can
have 4 distinct codewords.

00:59:43.610 --> 00:59:45.350
So now doing this
thing basically

00:59:45.350 --> 00:59:47.690
allowed you to increase
the number of codewords

00:59:47.690 --> 00:59:48.605
which you can have.

00:59:51.840 --> 00:59:57.150
More generally, so 4 kind of
comes from, again, this 1 bit--

00:59:57.150 --> 00:59:59.790
oh, sorry--

00:59:59.790 --> 01:00:04.080
this 1 bit per symbol and 2
symbol per code vector, which

01:00:04.080 --> 01:00:06.780
basically gives you 4.

01:00:06.780 --> 01:00:10.950
Generalizing this exact thing,
so let's say you have vectors--

01:00:10.950 --> 01:00:13.890
or if you are more comfortable
with the idea of blocks, blocks

01:00:13.890 --> 01:00:18.090
which are of size k, and your
rate is R bits per symbol,

01:00:18.090 --> 01:00:22.880
then your codebook is of
the size 2 to R times k.

01:00:22.880 --> 01:00:24.890
It's like generalizing
the exact same thing.

01:00:32.000 --> 01:00:35.630
In other words, your rate
is just-- again, sorry,

01:00:35.630 --> 01:00:36.890
there should be--

01:00:36.890 --> 01:00:40.820
yeah, there should be ceil
everywhere, so I apologize.

01:00:40.820 --> 01:00:45.080
But I hope you guys understand
the ceil part for now.

01:00:45.080 --> 01:00:51.020
Your rate [AUDIO OUT] per symbol
if you have an N-size codeword.

