01:07:23.700 --> 01:07:28.260
OK, so more formally, what did
we do in vector quantization?

01:07:28.260 --> 01:07:30.660
So this is, again,
just generalization

01:07:30.660 --> 01:07:34.890
of what we saw before.

01:07:34.890 --> 01:07:37.500
So your vector quantizer
is just a mapping

01:07:37.500 --> 01:07:41.250
from some k dimensional space to
a codebook, where a codebook is

01:07:41.250 --> 01:07:43.110
N dimensional vectors.

01:07:43.110 --> 01:07:45.300
So it's a dictionary
which is comprising

01:07:45.300 --> 01:07:48.300
of N k-dimensional vectors.

01:07:48.300 --> 01:07:50.730
Then you have a mapping
from this vector

01:07:50.730 --> 01:07:54.930
x, which is in k dimension,
your function Q to y bar.

01:07:54.930 --> 01:08:01.780
And then, where if x belongs to
some partition of this space,

01:08:01.780 --> 01:08:04.200
you will basically map
it to the i-th codeword.

01:08:04.200 --> 01:08:10.200
So for example here,
in this example,

01:08:10.200 --> 01:08:13.230
this was one partition
of the space.

01:08:13.230 --> 01:08:17.310
This was another
partition of the space.

01:08:17.310 --> 01:08:18.960
And so this is just
mathematically.

01:08:18.960 --> 01:08:23.910
So your partition should
span the whole space

01:08:23.910 --> 01:08:26.550
so that any vector which
is coming in that space

01:08:26.550 --> 01:08:29.196
should be mapped to
some quantized value.

01:08:29.196 --> 01:08:30.779
Otherwise, you can
have a symbol which

01:08:30.779 --> 01:08:33.540
you don't know where to map to.

01:08:33.540 --> 01:08:36.210
And then in this case, we saw
that the rate will be just

01:08:36.210 --> 01:08:37.995
log N by k bits per sample.

01:08:40.700 --> 01:08:42.920
Good?

01:08:42.920 --> 01:08:44.810
So I want to highlight
a couple of things,

01:08:44.810 --> 01:08:48.040
like vector quantization.

01:08:48.040 --> 01:08:50.859
So basically, the kind of
benefits we can exploit

01:08:50.859 --> 01:08:54.040
is that it can obviously exploit
the dependence between vector

01:08:54.040 --> 01:08:57.160
components, so that's
what we are working with.

01:08:57.160 --> 01:09:00.550
I'll give you an example of
this thing really quickly.

01:09:00.550 --> 01:09:03.700
And another thing is we can
have much more general decision

01:09:03.700 --> 01:09:05.290
regions.

01:09:05.290 --> 01:09:08.020
So to give an example
of both of these things,

01:09:08.020 --> 01:09:11.620
let's say you had some
distribution X1, X2 in 2D space.

01:09:11.620 --> 01:09:16.240
Instead of Gaussian, let's say
it looks something like this.

01:09:16.240 --> 01:09:18.610
And this just basically
means it's like X1,

01:09:18.610 --> 01:09:20.410
X2 always have the same sign.

01:09:20.410 --> 01:09:22.330
If X1 is positive,
X2 is positive.

01:09:22.330 --> 01:09:28.330
If X1 is negative, x2 is
negative, so on and so forth.

01:09:28.330 --> 01:09:30.370
If you were to do
a uniform scalar

01:09:30.370 --> 01:09:34.270
quantization of this
thing, what were you doing?

01:09:34.270 --> 01:09:38.920
You were basically making a
grid along x and a grid along y.

01:09:38.920 --> 01:09:42.609
And your decision regions would
have looked like these blocks

01:09:42.609 --> 01:09:45.140
here.

01:09:45.140 --> 01:09:47.210
And in this particular
example, you

01:09:47.210 --> 01:09:50.285
can work it out since
I have 36 blocks.

01:09:54.250 --> 01:09:59.560
For vector of size 2, it's
approximately log 2 the base 6.

01:09:59.560 --> 01:10:01.620
This is your rate.

01:10:01.620 --> 01:10:06.030
But obviously, if I knew this
fact that my distribution always

01:10:06.030 --> 01:10:08.580
is correlated, like X1,
X2 are always together,

01:10:08.580 --> 01:10:11.220
positive or negative, I
could have instead done

01:10:11.220 --> 01:10:12.630
vector quantization.

01:10:12.630 --> 01:10:15.570
And I could have only
defined my regions

01:10:15.570 --> 01:10:18.060
as these square
blocks here, which

01:10:18.060 --> 01:10:21.810
overlap with my actual density.

01:10:21.810 --> 01:10:25.470
In that case, I don't need
all the different codewords

01:10:25.470 --> 01:10:28.760
corresponding to the other side.

01:10:28.760 --> 01:10:31.020
And in this case, you
can again work it out.

01:10:31.020 --> 01:10:33.800
But it's just
basically log 18 by 2.

01:10:33.800 --> 01:10:40.960
So that's your bits, which is
I think something like this.

01:10:40.960 --> 01:10:45.420
So in this case, you can save
half a bit for, it's obvious,

01:10:45.420 --> 01:10:50.630
same distortion because we
have the exact same regions

01:10:50.630 --> 01:10:52.700
in the decision regions.

01:10:52.700 --> 01:10:54.590
So this is how
vector quantization

01:10:54.590 --> 01:10:55.970
can be really helpful.

01:10:55.970 --> 01:10:58.490
So it can help you
exploit correlation.

01:11:02.310 --> 01:11:06.810
But actually, it's much
stronger than that.

01:11:06.810 --> 01:11:09.060
And we'll not cover
this in class.

01:11:09.060 --> 01:11:12.150
Again, this goes into a little
bit of signal processing.

01:11:12.150 --> 01:11:16.260
But you can work out some
comments without proofs.

01:11:16.260 --> 01:11:18.945
And I think we have some
proofs again in the notes.

01:11:18.945 --> 01:11:21.070
So if you are-- if you are
mathematically inclined,

01:11:21.070 --> 01:11:22.445
interested, I
would encourage you

01:11:22.445 --> 01:11:24.340
to go read them, work them out.

01:11:24.340 --> 01:11:26.760
But basically, what you can
show that optimal regions are

01:11:26.760 --> 01:11:29.610
generally not uniform,
which is basically

01:11:29.610 --> 01:11:34.590
saying scalar quantization,
even in simple uniform IID case.

01:11:34.590 --> 01:11:36.720
So in previous example, it was--

01:11:36.720 --> 01:11:38.160
you had correlated sources.

01:11:38.160 --> 01:11:41.520
But what now I'm saying is you
can show that mathematically.

01:11:41.520 --> 01:11:44.190
Like, if you were to break
it down into square grids,

01:11:44.190 --> 01:11:48.210
that's not optimal even
for uniform IID case.

01:11:48.210 --> 01:11:53.190
And the idea really is you can
have an hexagonal lattice, so

01:11:53.190 --> 01:11:53.850
a uniform--

01:11:57.350 --> 01:12:02.650
a uniform encoder
really is what it's--

01:12:02.650 --> 01:12:03.415
let's see, sorry.

01:12:08.350 --> 01:12:14.770
So a uniform encoder
is grids like this,

01:12:14.770 --> 01:12:18.490
make square grids like this,
something for uniform IID scalar

01:12:18.490 --> 01:12:19.510
quantization.

01:12:23.410 --> 01:12:25.780
Again, so I have determined
some decision regions.

01:12:25.780 --> 01:12:28.300
I have determined the
codebook contains values.

01:12:28.300 --> 01:12:31.000
What I'm saying is
there is another grid.

01:12:31.000 --> 01:12:37.780
So if I take my quantize values
as shifted half from each other,

01:12:37.780 --> 01:12:39.520
like somewhere--
so each next row

01:12:39.520 --> 01:12:41.350
is in between the
next one, something

01:12:41.350 --> 01:12:45.220
like a honeycomb structure,
and if these were my decision

01:12:45.220 --> 01:12:49.540
regions, you can work
out that, I don't know,

01:12:49.540 --> 01:12:53.050
the region corresponding to one
thing would look like hexagonal.

01:12:53.050 --> 01:12:56.680
And this vector
quantizer is actually

01:12:56.680 --> 01:12:58.300
better than the first one.

01:12:58.300 --> 01:13:00.310
And you can work out the math.

01:13:00.310 --> 01:13:04.480
And it basically turns out
to be-- like for same rate,

01:13:04.480 --> 01:13:05.830
it turns out to be--

01:13:05.830 --> 01:13:09.550
your MSE turns out to
be around 4% lower.

01:13:12.370 --> 01:13:13.690
This is a good idea to know.

01:13:13.690 --> 01:13:17.020
And this is called lattice
quantization or Voronoi

01:13:17.020 --> 01:13:18.760
diagrams.

01:13:18.760 --> 01:13:21.160
And you can also accommodate
more than two dimensions.

01:13:21.160 --> 01:13:23.980
So this is just a hexagonal
lattice in two dimension.

01:13:23.980 --> 01:13:25.660
But it's actually--
now you can see

01:13:25.660 --> 01:13:27.790
you can get very
creative with respect

01:13:27.790 --> 01:13:29.860
to this lattice design
and your distortion.

01:13:29.860 --> 01:13:31.540
And that can actually
do much better

01:13:31.540 --> 01:13:35.740
than just doing
scalar quantization.

01:13:35.740 --> 01:13:41.680
In fact, this paper, Ziv,
on universal quantization--

01:13:41.680 --> 01:13:45.340
OK, sorry, before that, so there
are two comments here you see.

01:13:45.340 --> 01:13:50.650
One is that, obviously, vector
quantization helps, even

01:13:50.650 --> 01:13:52.750
in the most trivial of cases.

01:13:52.750 --> 01:13:56.740
But the second thing
is it helps, but is it

01:13:56.740 --> 01:13:58.813
like scalar uniform quantizer?

01:13:58.813 --> 01:13:59.980
I don't have to do anything.

01:13:59.980 --> 01:14:02.200
I just divided my
thing and fixed bits,

01:14:02.200 --> 01:14:03.700
didn't have to think anything?

01:14:03.700 --> 01:14:06.100
Like, 32-bit floats,
that's my scalar vector

01:14:06.100 --> 01:14:08.710
quantized-- scalar quantizer,
uniform scalar quantizer.

01:14:08.710 --> 01:14:10.880
This one I had to
figure out, oh, this

01:14:10.880 --> 01:14:12.890
is how I need to
work out my vectors.

01:14:12.890 --> 01:14:15.170
This needs to be the letters.

01:14:15.170 --> 01:14:20.510
And this is the optimal one in
that it gives like 4% benefit.

01:14:20.510 --> 01:14:24.120
So the takeaway really is that--

01:14:24.120 --> 01:14:29.840
and this is a paper by Ziv,
who is like same Lempel Ziv.

01:14:29.840 --> 01:14:32.870
And here he shows that
for certain cases--

01:14:32.870 --> 01:14:34.070
we'll not go into details--

01:14:34.070 --> 01:14:38.600
for certain cases, you can show
that scalar quantizer doesn't

01:14:38.600 --> 01:14:41.960
take more than 0.754
bits per sample

01:14:41.960 --> 01:14:44.540
higher than vector
for any design.

01:14:47.100 --> 01:14:53.220
OK, so in general, your optimal
regions are not easy to compute

01:14:53.220 --> 01:14:56.220
and may even give
you very less value

01:14:56.220 --> 01:14:58.740
addition to whatever
quantization scheme

01:14:58.740 --> 01:15:00.580
you are designing.

01:15:00.580 --> 01:15:03.220
So you need to be
careful when designing

01:15:03.220 --> 01:15:05.320
on how much computational
complexity am

01:15:05.320 --> 01:15:08.500
I going to increase if I'm
designing a practical compressor

01:15:08.500 --> 01:15:09.838
around vector quantization.

01:15:09.838 --> 01:15:12.130
So you can see it's like the
same idea around the block

01:15:12.130 --> 01:15:12.660
codes.

01:15:12.660 --> 01:15:13.660
Block codes are amazing.

01:15:13.660 --> 01:15:15.118
They'll give you
great performance,

01:15:15.118 --> 01:15:16.360
great theoretical guarantees.

01:15:16.360 --> 01:15:19.810
But they're exponential
in terms of complexity.

01:15:19.810 --> 01:15:24.670
And that really makes them
rare in practice relatively.

01:15:24.670 --> 01:15:26.410
And we had to come
around workarounds.

01:15:26.410 --> 01:15:29.350
Things similar is the case
with vector quantization.

01:15:33.910 --> 01:15:35.950
OK, so just the last thing.

01:15:35.950 --> 01:15:37.390
