01:16:26.960 --> 01:16:29.105
before, this vector
quantization idea?

01:16:36.980 --> 01:16:38.810
Think about what we
are trying to do.

01:16:45.300 --> 01:16:46.120
Sorry?

01:16:46.120 --> 01:16:46.620
Clustering.

01:16:46.620 --> 01:16:47.430
Clustering.

01:16:47.430 --> 01:16:48.300
OK, exactly.

01:16:48.300 --> 01:16:51.180
So one of the students
said clustering.

01:16:51.180 --> 01:16:53.550
So we are exactly
doing clustering.

01:16:53.550 --> 01:16:55.320
Given the sample
points, we are trying

01:16:55.320 --> 01:16:57.840
to figure out the decision
regions, clusters,

01:16:57.840 --> 01:17:03.400
in other words, under which like
we have one quantized value.

01:17:03.400 --> 01:17:05.250
So if you know about
clustering, you

01:17:05.250 --> 01:17:08.190
would have heard of the k-means
algorithm in one of your machine

01:17:08.190 --> 01:17:09.810
learning class.

01:17:09.810 --> 01:17:13.050
And basically,
[INAUDIBLE] is very

01:17:13.050 --> 01:17:14.640
similar to k-means algorithm.

01:17:14.640 --> 01:17:17.070
In fact, it's the same thing.

01:17:17.070 --> 01:17:21.150
It's also called Lloyd-Max
algorithm or Generalized Lloyd

01:17:21.150 --> 01:17:24.870
algorithm if you are coming from
a compression angle or signal

01:17:24.870 --> 01:17:26.400
processing or quantization way.

01:17:26.400 --> 01:17:29.070
But if you witnessed ML
and clustering before,

01:17:29.070 --> 01:17:31.710
you would have seen
this as k-means.

01:17:31.710 --> 01:17:34.740
And k-means is just a special
case of Generalized Lloyd.

01:17:34.740 --> 01:17:39.240
Generalized Lloyd is a bit more
general, as the name suggests.

01:17:39.240 --> 01:17:41.160
And we are not going
to look into it.

01:17:41.160 --> 01:17:44.310
But basically, if you want
to-- so the thing which I say,

01:17:44.310 --> 01:17:48.920
if you want to cluster your
data points into N clusters

01:17:48.920 --> 01:17:51.140
corresponding to
the codebook-- so we

01:17:51.140 --> 01:17:54.060
had the endpoints
in the codebook.

01:17:54.060 --> 01:17:58.740
You can think of them as N
clusters, which is basically

01:17:58.740 --> 01:18:00.090
the same as k in k-means.

01:18:02.930 --> 01:18:05.720
And you choose these points
such that the average distortion

01:18:05.720 --> 01:18:06.710
is minimized.

01:18:09.260 --> 01:18:12.590
And this is just like
a fun historical note,

01:18:12.590 --> 01:18:14.042
which you can use.

01:18:14.042 --> 01:18:15.500
It's actually a
very old algorithm.

01:18:15.500 --> 01:18:19.190
It wasn't even
published in 1982.

01:18:19.190 --> 01:18:22.420
And basically
Generalized Lloyd, which

01:18:22.420 --> 01:18:23.920
is specialized to
the squared error,

01:18:23.920 --> 01:18:25.750
is the k-means
clustering algorithm,

01:18:25.750 --> 01:18:28.160
which you would have seen.

01:18:28.160 --> 01:18:30.260
And so the main idea
of k-means clustering

01:18:30.260 --> 01:18:33.690
is given some data
point [AUDIO OUT]

01:18:33.690 --> 01:18:36.840
codebook and the corresponding
partition of the data points.

01:18:36.840 --> 01:18:38.580
So you want to figure out--

01:18:38.580 --> 01:18:40.860
I have been told what's
my N. I have been

01:18:40.860 --> 01:18:42.180
told the size of my codebook.

01:18:42.180 --> 01:18:43.710
So in other words,
rate is given.

01:18:43.710 --> 01:18:47.250
Now I need to figure out what
should be my optimal decision

01:18:47.250 --> 01:18:50.400
regions and the quantized
values, clusters,

01:18:50.400 --> 01:18:51.780
and decision boundaries.

01:18:51.780 --> 01:18:55.440
Now, how k-means work is you
basically do it iteratively.

01:18:55.440 --> 01:18:57.480
So given a codebook,
you basically

01:18:57.480 --> 01:19:00.360
compute the best partition
for the data points.

01:19:00.360 --> 01:19:02.070
And then given a
partition of the data

01:19:02.070 --> 01:19:04.260
points, you compute
the optimal codebook.

01:19:04.260 --> 01:19:06.750
And then you keep repeating
until convergence.

01:19:10.960 --> 01:19:12.580
So we don't have
time, so I'm not

01:19:12.580 --> 01:19:15.290
going to go into the
algorithmic details,

01:19:15.290 --> 01:19:17.260
but there is some
pseudocode here.

01:19:17.260 --> 01:19:19.420
And the idea is,
again, very simple.

01:19:19.420 --> 01:19:21.820
It's quite detailed, so
we can-- you can go over.

01:19:21.820 --> 01:19:24.440
But you start with some
centroids, which are,

01:19:24.440 --> 01:19:29.440
other words, your quantized
values, till some convergence.

01:19:29.440 --> 01:19:31.120
You first determine clusters.

01:19:31.120 --> 01:19:35.630
So you basically assign each
symbol or data to some centroid.

01:19:35.630 --> 01:19:40.280
And then, given these clusters,
you compute the new centroids.

01:19:40.280 --> 01:19:42.710
And you keep doing this till
some convergence is hit.

01:19:42.710 --> 01:19:44.870
And that gives you these
clusters and centroids.

01:19:44.870 --> 01:19:48.590
And something to just
remember, don't get confused, k

01:19:48.590 --> 01:19:50.990
is the size of the codebook.

01:19:50.990 --> 01:19:52.880
K is the number of
clusters in k-means,

01:19:52.880 --> 01:19:59.840
which we have been calling N.
So just k in our discussion

01:19:59.840 --> 01:20:01.655
so far was the size
of the vectors.

01:20:05.370 --> 01:20:08.880
And so you can go over this.

01:20:08.880 --> 01:20:12.105
Just quickly want to show
there is this notebook.

